    "cultural_notes_vi": "Korean reception history tradition: (1) 수용미학 (Iser, Jauss) introduced 1980s via 김주연, 김현; (2) Korean 정전 formation: 1980-90s critical effort to define 한국 현대문학 canon; debated by 황종연 ('canon as institutional'); (3) 박경리 「토지」 reception is exemplary case study — well-documented across 4 decades; (4) Major reception history projects: 「창작과비평」 50주년 회고 (2016), 「문학과지성」 50주년 (2020); (5) Database resources: RISS (riss.kr), KCI (kci.go.kr) for academic reception; KISS (한국 학술지 인용 색인) for citation analysis; (6) Vietnamese learner: reception history particularly rich field for Korean literature because rapid modernization compressed many reception phases into one century — same novel can have totally different meanings in 1970s vs 2020s; (7) Reception of Korean lit abroad: 한강 'Vegetarian' Booker 2016 transformed Korean lit's global reception almost overnight — case study in cross-cultural reception; (8) Korean institutional reception markers: 문학상 (literary prizes — 이상문학상, 동인문학상), 교과서 등재 (textbook inclusion), 학회 발표 (conference presentations) all systematic indicators.",
    "tip_advice_vi": "Reception history 5-step: (1) PERIODIZE — divide work's afterlife into 3-5 distinct periods based on sociopolitical / critical shifts; (2) SAMPLE diverse sources per period — don't rely only on prestigious critics; include popular reviews, sales data, classroom usage, citation patterns; (3) IDENTIFY shifts — what changed in interpretation between periods; (4) EXPLAIN shifts — what historical / critical movements drove the change; (5) ANALYZE silences — periods of critical neglect are themselves data. C2 best practices: (a) Distinguish 'work changed' from 'reader changed' — almost always reader; (b) Be wary of nostalgic 'first readers were closer to work'; (c) Track institutional reception (textbooks, awards) separately from critical reception (journals); (d) Note reception across borders — international vs domestic. Sai lầm thường gặp: (a) Treating reception as linear progress (prior readers were 'wrong', now we know better); (b) Privileging current reception as authoritative; (c) Confusing critical reception with popular reception; (d) Forgetting that critic's own historical position shapes their reception study.",
