    "cultural_notes_vi": "Korean theoretical reception: (1) 1980s — Marxism (đặc biệt Lukács, Goldmann) dominant in literary criticism, allied với 민족문학론 ('national literature theory'); (2) Late 1980s — feminism arrives via 영문학과 (English-lit dept), 정희진 và others; (3) 1990s — post-structuralism (Barthes, Foucault, Derrida) translated; reception cooler than France/US; (4) 2000s — postcolonialism (Spivak, Bhabha) heavily debated re 식민지 vs 해방 frame; (5) 2010s — ecocriticism rising với 한강, 김초엽 generation; trauma theory deepens với 5·18 / Sewol commemoration; (6) Korean theoretical scene: prefer mid-range theory over grand theory — Foucault, Bourdieu more cited than Heidegger / Hegel; (7) 사자성어: 격물치지 (格物致知 — investigate things to gain knowledge — Confucian principle, oft-invoked in critical method debates); (8) Vietnamese learner: Korean theoretical scene similar to Vietnamese in 1990s onwards reception of post-structuralism; differences: Korea heavier in feminism, Vietnam heavier in Marxist literary tradition. Korean and Vietnamese share complicated relations với postcolonial frame because of Cold War alignments.",
    "tip_advice_vi": "Theoretical lens selection 5-step: (1) ASK what textual question you're answering — theory is a tool, not master; (2) IDENTIFY which theories text 'speaks to' — does text foreground class, gender, race, ecology, psyche, language? (3) CHOOSE primary lens với explicit justification — why this theory for this text; (4) IDENTIFY supplementary lenses — what does primary lens miss; (5) ACKNOWLEDGE text's resistance to your chosen theory — every theory leaves residue. C2 best practices: (a) Avoid 'theory-shopping' (picking trendy theory regardless of fit); (b) Read theorists in their context — Marxism in 1844 ≠ Marxism in 1968 ≠ Marxism in 2020; (c) Acknowledge that 'no theory' is also a theory (often liberal humanism); (d) Watch for over-application — sometimes text resists the theory and that resistance is the finding. Sai lầm thường gặp: (a) Theory as decoration not analytic — citing Foucault without using Foucault; (b) Picking theory to match conclusion already wanted; (c) Treating theory as universally applicable — situate; (d) Forgetting that 'apply theory X to work Y' format is itself a critical convention. Pluralism rule: prefer 병치 (juxtaposition) over forced 통합 (synthesis); let lenses produce different visible aspects, don't reduce.",
